          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The phrase denotes profound darkness associated with death, mortal peril, severe affliction, or spiritual alienation, often emphasizing God’s presence or deliverance into light.\",\"book_summary\":\"The phrase specifically marks the border of Death’s mountain-kingdom; Psalm 23’s valley is a passage through that boundary from Death’s realm of darkness into Christ’s kingdom of light and life.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Death-associated darkness or mortal peril.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The boundary of Death’s kingdom traversed in passage to Christ’s kingdom.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus covers dying, mortal danger, suffering, captivity, gloom, Sheol, and spiritual darkness. The book restricts the symbol to a kingdom-border interpretation and reinterprets Psalm 23’s valley as passage between opposed mountain-kingdoms, excluding ordinary instances of danger or despair unless they participate in that transition.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no necessary contradiction: a boundary of Death’s realm can remain death-associated darkness and peril. The disagreement is that the book treats this specific spatial and kingdom structure as the symbol’s identity rather than as one possible application.\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the consensus category of deathly darkness and deliverance into divine light, especially the spiritual-darkness strand of Isaiah 9, Luke 1, and Colossians 1. It materially narrows that category by identifying the shadow as a precise border around Death’s mountain-kingdom. This resembles the recognized ‘realm or borderland of death’ alternative, so it is not novel, but its mountain-kingdom topology is substantially more restrictive than the consensus.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The cited texts separately associate the shadow with darkness and deliverance, valleys with space between mountains, mountains with kingdoms, and salvation with transfer from darkness to Christ’s kingdom. None explicitly identifies Death as a mountain-kingdom or states that its shadow is the kingdom’s boundary, so the central synthesis depends on cross-textual symbolic inference.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
